Given: $\sec xdy + \left(2\left(1 - x\right)\tan x + x\left(2 - x\right)\right)dx = 0 \implies \sec xdy = \left(2\left(x - 1\right)\tan x + \left({x}^{2} - 2x\right)\right)dx \implies \frac{dy}{\cos x} = \left(2\left(x - 1\right)\frac{\sin x}{\cos x} + \left({x}^{2} - 2x\right)\right)dx \implies dy = \left(2\left(x - 1\right)\sin x + \left({x}^{2} - 2x\right)\cos x\right)dx \implies \int dy = \int \left(2\left(x - 1\right)\sin x + \left({x}^{2} - 2x\right)\cos x\right)dx \implies \int dy = \int 2\left(x - 1\right)\sin x + \int \left({x}^{2} - 2x\right)\cos xdx \implies y(x) = \int 2(x - 1)\sin xdx + \left(\left({x}^{2} - 2x\right)(\sin x) - \int (2x - 2)\sin xdx\right) \implies y(x) = \left({x}^{2} - 2x\right)\sin x + \lambda$

It is given that, $y\left(0\right) = 2 \implies y(0) = \left(0 - 0\right)\sin 0 + \lambda \implies 2 = 0 + \lambda \implies \lambda = 2 \implies y(x) = \left({x}^{2} - 2x\right)\sin x + 2 \implies y(2) = \left({2}^{2} - 2 \times 2\right)\sin \left(2\right) + 2 \implies y\left(2\right) = 2$

